{"page":220,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.20386,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"206 Chapter 3: Derivatives"},{"x":0.56063,"y":0.09819,"w":0.04273,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = f(x)"},{"x":0.4826,"y":0.12474,"w":0.10161,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(a + dx, f(a + dx))"},{"x":0.65593,"y":0.14472,"w":0.12043,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Δy = f(a + dx) −f(a)"},{"x":0.59988,"y":0.17646,"w":0.07168,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ΔL = f′(a)dx"},{"x":0.47432,"y":0.19232,"w":0.10737,"h":0.02483,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"dx = Δx (a, f(a))"},{"x":0.60923,"y":0.21933,"w":0.16676,"h":0.0348,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"When dx is a small change in x, the corresponding change in the linearization is precisely dy."},{"x":0.45378,"y":0.23686,"w":0.04289,"h":0.02251,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Tangent line"},{"x":0.57992,"y":0.27268,"w":0.037,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a + dx"},{"x":0.43396,"y":0.2732,"w":0.08899,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 a"},{"x":0.42802,"y":0.29687,"w":0.38354,"h":0.04297,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 3.55 Geometrically, the differential dy is the change ∆L in the linearization of ƒ when x = a changes by an amount dx = ∆x."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.36852,"w":0.593,"h":0.0435,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"That is, the change in the linearization of ƒ is precisely the value of the differential dy when x = a and dx = ∆x. Therefore, dy represents the amount the tangent line rises or falls when x changes by an amount dx = ∆x."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.4139,"w":0.59291,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"If dx ≠0, then the quotient of the differential dy by the differential dx is equal to the derivative ƒ′(x) because"},{"x":0.50151,"y":0.44955,"w":0.13081,"h":0.02296,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"dy , dx = ƒ′(x) dx"},{"x":0.59898,"y":0.45006,"w":0.12932,"h":0.02973,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"dx = ƒ′(x) = dy"},{"x":0.71287,"y":0.45973,"w":0.0212,"h":0.02006,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dx."},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.49042,"w":0.13318,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"We sometimes write"},{"x":0.57179,"y":0.51276,"w":0.09192,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dƒ = ƒ′(x) dx"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.5313,"w":0.58812,"h":0.03329,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"in place of dy = ƒ′(x) dx, calling dƒ the differential of ƒ. For instance, if ƒ(x) = 3x2 - 6, then"},{"x":0.52933,"y":0.56967,"w":0.17694,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"dƒ = d(3x2 - 6) = 6x dx."},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.59785,"w":0.22044,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Every differentiation formula like"},{"x":0.4406,"y":0.61892,"w":0.06236,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"d(u + y)"},{"x":0.56906,"y":0.61958,"w":0.14684,"h":0.0294,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"dx or d(sin u)"},{"x":0.52795,"y":0.62104,"w":0.05722,"h":0.02794,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dx + dy"},{"x":0.46408,"y":0.62171,"w":0.07974,"h":0.02728,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"dx = du"},{"x":0.68199,"y":0.62171,"w":0.113,"h":0.02727,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"dx = cos u du"},{"x":0.77906,"y":0.6362,"w":0.01543,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dx"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.65834,"w":0.27156,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"has a corresponding differential form like"},{"x":0.44103,"y":0.68068,"w":0.35352,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"d(u + y) = du + dy or d(sin u) = cos u du."},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.71802,"w":0.58902,"h":0.02984,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 5 We can use the Chain Rule and other differentiation rules to find differ- entials of functions."},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.75362,"w":0.31134,"h":0.01727,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(a) d(tan 2x) = sec2(2x) d(2x) = 2sec2 2x dx"},{"x":0.32334,"y":0.77745,"w":0.29255,"h":0.03044,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"(b) d a x x + 1b = (x + 1) dx - x d(x + 1)"},{"x":0.50447,"y":0.78001,"w":0.25352,"h":0.02957,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"(x + 1)2 = x dx + dx - x dx"},{"x":0.67107,"y":0.78068,"w":0.16756,"h":0.0289,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"(x + 1)2 = dx (x + 1)2"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.82846,"w":0.21882,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Estimating with Differentials"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.8531,"w":0.59302,"h":0.05885,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Suppose we know the value of a differentiable function ƒ(x) at a point a and want to esti- mate how much this value will change if we move to a nearby point a + dx. If dx = ∆x is small, then we can see from Figure 3.55 that ∆y is approximately equal to the differential dy. Since"},{"x":0.49332,"y":0.9215,"w":0.24888,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(a + dx) = ƒ(a) + ∆y, ∆x = dx"}]}